The conductivity of a solution is measured with a conductometer (see Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-conductometer}).
The principle behind a conductometer is simple. Conductivity is measured by measuring the resistance offered by the solution within two electrodes inside a conductivity cell.
A Wheatstone bridge-type set up with alternating current is used to measure this resistence.
The conductivity cell inside conductometers is made of a glass with two electrodes made of coated platinum. Conductivity cells are characterized by a cell constant $K_{cell}$ that depends on the length and area of the cell.
\[
K_{cell}=\frac{A}{l}
\]

 

